    "problem_statement": "# Addition operator (`+`) broken for Dict objects\n\nI found an issue with the `+` operator for Dict objects. When trying to add dictionaries, it's not working as expected.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```python\nfrom addict import Dict\n\n# Case 1: Adding an empty Dict\nempty_dict = Dict()\nother_dict = Dict(a=1, b=2)\nresult = empty_dict + other_dict  # This should return other_dict but fails\n\n# Case 2: Adding non-empty Dicts\ndict1 = Dict(a=1, b=2)\ndict2 = Dict(c=3, d=4)\nresult = dict1 + dict2  # This should combine the dictionaries but fails\n```\n\nBoth cases result in an AttributeError. The first case should return the non-empty dictionary when adding to an empty dictionary, and the second case should properly handle addition of two non-empty dictionaries.\n\nAdditionally, when dictionaries have the same keys, the behavior is incorrect. It returns the first dictionary instead of properly combining them.\n\nThe issue appears to be in the `__add__` method implementation, which has incorrect logic for handling empty dictionaries and also returns a TypeError object instead of raising it in some cases."
  },
